window.FEAT=[{"id": "nunavut", "tag": "Projects", "topic": "Projects", "title": "Teams shortlisted for $1B Nunavut port project", "dek": "Three construction teams advance in procurement for Grays Bay, a northern road-and-port corridor intended to connect mineral deposits with export markets.", "homeDek": "Three construction teams advance in procurement for Grays Bay, a northern road-and-port corridor intended to connect mineral deposits with export markets.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Sep 16, 2026", "img": "nunavut", "alt": "Teams shortlisted for $1B Nunavut port project", "credit": "Unsplash", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/teams-shortlisted-for-1b-nunavut-port-project/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["The teams are headlined by industry leads Kiewit, Ledcor/Bird, and Samsung C&T/Webuild.", "The $1-billion project features a 230 km all-season road and Arctic deepwater port at Coronation Gulf, acting as a critical corridor to unlock northern critical mineral deposits.", "WKR filed its environmental Impact Statement in March 2026, and Ottawa subsequently referred the project to its Major Projects Office."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "West Kitikmeot Resources Corp. (WKR) has shortlisted three major international construction consortiums to move forward in the bidding process for major works on the proposed Grays Bay Road and Port Project."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The announcement marks a key procurement milestone for the mega-infrastructure initiative aimed at connecting mineral-rich regions of Nunavut to global shipping routes and southern Canada."}, {"k": "p", "t": "WKR launched the public Request for Qualifications (RFQ) process on June 1, 2026. A Request for Proposals (RFP) package will be distributed to the three shortlisted respondents later this month, with WKR targeting the selection of a preferred proponent in March 2027."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three shortlisted respondents are:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["<strong>Kiewit Construction Services ULC</strong>, supported by sub-contractors North American Construction Group, Hatch, WSP, and Thurber Engineering Ltd.", "<strong>Arctic Passage Partners GP</strong> — a joint partnership between Ledcor and Bird Construction — supported by sub-contractors Parsons Corporation, Associated Engineering, Tetra Tech, Baird & Associates, PND Engineers, Inc., and Alietum Ice Inc.", "<strong>Grays Bay Infrastructure Partners</strong> — a joint partnership between Samsung C&T, Engineering & Construction and Webuild S.p.A. — supported by sub-contractors Jacobs and CIMA+."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "WKR, an Inuit-owned entity, is leading the development of the multi-user infrastructure corridor.<sup></sup> The proposed project features a 230-kilometre all-season access road connecting the Arctic coast to Jericho Station, adjacent to the northern terminus of the Tibbitt to Contwoyto Winter Road. At its northern terminal, the initiative includes a deepwater ocean port and small craft harbour along the Coronation Gulf on the Northwest Passage, alongside an airstrip, fuel storage, and logistics facilities designed to unlock critical mineral deposits like copper and zinc while enhancing northern supply chains and national security."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The project continues to advance through key federal and regulatory review stages. WKR submitted its formal Impact Statement to the Nunavut Impact Review Board on March 1, 2026, launching an environmental assessment grounded in traditional Inuit knowledge and ecological monitoring.<sup></sup> Following the project’s referral to the federal Major Projects Office in March, Ottawa initiated public consultations in June to consider listing the corridor under Schedule 1 of the <em>Building Canada Act</em> to streamline federal permitting post-environmental review."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To support ongoing pre-construction and engineering development, the Government of Canada conditionally approved up to $50 million in May through the First and Last Mile Fund, building on early development support from federal and territorial partners.<sup></sup> Major construction is expected to span approximately five years following final regulatory approvals and financial close."}]}, {"id": "ontline", "tag": "Projects", "topic": "Projects", "title": "Consortium wins $4.32B Ontario Line tunnelling contract", "dek": "Pape North Connect will deliver tunnels and underground stations for Toronto’s Ontario Line, with Webuild and FCC Canada leading construction.", "homeDek": "Pape North Connect will deliver tunnels and underground stations for Toronto’s Ontario Line, with Webuild and FCC Canada leading construction.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Aug 7, 2026", "img": "ontline", "alt": "Consortium wins $4.32B Ontario Line tunnelling contract", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/consortium-wins-4-32b-ontario-line-tunnelling-contract/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["Metrolinx has finalized a $4.32-billion Target Price Agreement with Pape North Connect to build the Pape Tunnel and Underground Stations package.", "Delivery will be led by Webuild and FCC Canada, supported by engineering partners Arcadis and AECOM.", "The project covers 3 km of twin tunneling under Pape Avenue, two underground stations (Pape and Cosburn), and underpinning for TTC Line 2 subway connection.", "Delivered via a Progressive Design-Build (PDB) framework to establish target pricing, reduce risks, and minimize disruption across Toronto’s east end."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Metrolinx and Infrastructure Ontario have signed a $4.32-billion Target Price Agreement with consortium Pape North Connect to deliver the Pape Tunnel and Underground Stations (PTUS) contract for the Ontario Line. The award follows the completion of a collaborative development phase under a Progressive Design-Build (PDB) delivery model, during which Metrolinx and the consortium refined designs, schedules, early works, and capital cost estimates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pape North Connect team includes construction partners Webuild Civil Work Inc. and Fomento de Construcciones y Contratas (FCC) Canada Ltd., along with design and engineering partners Arcadis Professional Services (Canada) Inc. and AECOM Canada Ltd. The contract represents one of the most complex underground engineering packages for the 15.6-kilometre rapid transit line, which will ultimately connect Exhibition Place and Ontario Place to the Ontario Science Centre."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“The target price refers to a jointly determined estimate of the total capital cost required to deliver the contract,” Metrolinx noted in the announcement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Progressive Design-Build model aims to mitigate cost overruns, optimize risk allocation, and improve schedule reliability before full-scale construction begins in a densely populated urban corridor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The underground package includes major civil, tunneling, and transit integration infrastructure beneath Pape Avenue:<sup></sup>"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["<strong>Twin Tunnels:</strong> Construction of 3 kilometres of twin tunnels extending between the Gerrard portal and the Minton portal near the Don Valley Bridge.", "<strong>New Underground Stations:</strong> Construction of two underground subway stations at Pape and Cosburn.", "<strong>Transit Integration:</strong> Underpinning and direct interface with the existing TTC Line 2 subway at Pape Station to allow seamless passenger transfers.", "<strong>Auxiliary Infrastructure:</strong> Building two tunnel portals, three emergency exit/service buildings, and a rail switch/crossover section near Sammon Avenue."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "First announced by the Government of Ontario in 2019, the Ontario Line is a 15.6-kilometre, 15-station stand-alone rapid transit line designed to transform transit across Toronto. Running from Exhibition Place and Ontario Place in the southwest, through downtown Toronto, and northeast to the Ontario Science Centre at Eglinton Avenue and Don Mills Road, the $27-billion megaproject serves as a critical relief valve for the city’s overburdened transit system."}]}, {"id": "summit50", "tag": "Economy", "topic": "Economy", "title": "50 massive projects featured at the Canada Investment Summit", "dek": "SiteNews examines major Canadian investment opportunities spanning energy, transportation and mining as officials seek international capital for long-term development.", "homeDek": "SiteNews examines major Canadian investment opportunities spanning energy, transportation and mining as officials seek international capital for long-term development.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Sep 14, 2026", "img": "summit50", "alt": "50 massive projects featured at the Canada Investment Summit", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/50-massive-projects-featured-at-the-canada-investment-summit/", "b": [{"k": "p", "t": "Prime Minister Mark Carney is pitching Canada as a great place for global capital."}, {"k": "p", "t": "<a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/campaign/canada-investment-summit-2026.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Canada Investment Summit 2026</a>, taking place September 14–15 in Toronto, Ontario, is a first-of-its-kind gathering hosted by the Prime Minister alongside CPP Investments and PSP Investments to catalyze $1 trillion in total investment over five years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Designed as a practical forum to showcase Canada’s G7-leading banking stability, highly educated workforce, and vast free-trade network, the summit brings together global investors, Canadian CEOs, and public sector leaders to accelerate long-horizon capital into productive assets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By focusing on nation-building initiatives, high-tech sectors like quantum technology, and commercial opportunities, the event aims to drive job creation, enhance supply-chain integration, and solidify Canada’s position as a top-tier global destination for foreign direct investment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here are the 50 biggest projects being highlighted at the summit:"}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>50. PLS Project</strong> ($1.66 Billion CAD)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The PLS Project is an advanced high-grade uranium development centered on the Triple R deposit in Saskatchewan. The project has received environmental assessment approval, has its CNSC licence application under review, and is progressing FEED alongside Indigenous engagement agreements."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>49. Port Infrastructure Modernization Project</strong> ($1.67 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Port Infrastructure Modernization Project is an initiative to rehabilitate and optimize port assets, intermodal operations, and deep-water docks at the Port of Québec. It is currently in advanced planning and early execution."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>48. The Alberta Project</strong> ($1.76 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Alberta Project is a sustainable fuels and carbon removal initiative designed to convert forestry residuals into renewable natural gas. The project is actively in the FEED stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>47. Nicola Wind Energy Project</strong> ($1.89 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Nicola Wind Energy Project is a proposed 496 MW wind farm in British Columbia co-developed by Elemental Energy and the Upper Nicola Band. The project is currently in advanced-stage development."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>46. Manitoba Renewable Fuel Production Facility</strong> ($1.89 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Manitoba Renewable Fuel Production Facility is planned as Canada’s first large-scale, SAF-focused renewables plant, converting canola oil into sustainable aviation fuel and renewable diesel. The project is in the pre-construction phase."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>45. 10GW Integrated Gigafactory Campus</strong> ($1.90 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The 10GW Integrated Gigafactory Campus is a proposed clean energy manufacturing facility in Chatham-Kent, Ontario, focused on solar PV and battery energy storage systems. Official status details for the project are currently unavailable."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>44. Loki Flake Graphite Deposit</strong> ($1.94 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Loki Flake Graphite Deposit in northern Saskatchewan is a high-grade critical mineral project targeting commercial production by 2029. It is advancing through the Preliminary Economic Assessment and environmental baseline study stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>43. Wicheeda Rare Earth Project</strong> ($1.94 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Wicheeda Rare Earth Project in British Columbia is an advanced rare earths development aiming to supply a neodymium-praseodymium enriched mixed carbonate. The project is currently in the feasibility stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>42. Pine Point Mine Development Project</strong> ($2 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pine Point Mine Development Project is a zinc-lead redevelopment initiative situated on the south shore of Great Slave Lake in the Northwest Territories. An internal feasibility report was completed in 2026 to advance the development."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>41. Yellowhead Copper Project</strong> ($2 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Yellowhead Copper Project is an advanced open-pit copper development in British Columbia featuring a projected 25-year mine life. It is currently progressing through the pre-feasibility and permitting stages."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>40. New Terminal to Transship Iron Ore at the Port of Belledune</strong> ($2.01 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This New Brunswick project involves a two-phase marine terminal expansion with new loading infrastructure, storage, dredging, and a deep-water transshipment terminal for iron ore. It is currently in early-stage development."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>39. The Port Alberni Transshipment Hub (PATH)</strong> ($2.23 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Port Alberni Transshipment Hub is a proposed marine logistics facility in British Columbia designed as a hub-and-spoke Pacific gateway for container distribution. Pre-feasibility work for the project has been completed."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>38. The Grande Prairie Project</strong> ($2.40 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Grande Prairie Project redevelops an existing industrial site in Alberta into a brownfield AI data center campus using 150 MW of power from established cogeneration. The initiative is actively undergoing FEED and environmental permitting."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>37. Labrador West Transmission</strong> ($2.50 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Labrador West Transmission project entails building a 735 kV transmission line and terminal station to connect Churchill Falls to Labrador West. The grid enhancement project is currently in the FEED stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>36. Courant Project</strong> ($2.63 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Courant Project in Baie-Comeau, Quebec, is a proposed renewable ammonium nitrate production facility utilizing carbon-free hydrogen and hydroelectricity. The development is currently in the Front End Engineering Design (FEED) stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>35. Via TWO</strong> ($2.70 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Via TWO is a phased synthetic graphite production facility in St. Thomas, Ontario, designed to supply the North American battery supply chain. With FEED completed and detailed engineering underway, the project is advancing toward a Final Investment Decision."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>34. Crawford Nickel Project</strong> ($2.70 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Crawford Nickel Project in Ontario is an advanced nickel sulphide mining project expected to yield over 1.6 million tonnes of nickel over a 40-year mine life. The project is currently in the pre-construction phase."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>33. Alto Northern Alberta Projects 1, 2, 3</strong> ($2.82 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Alto Northern Alberta Projects 1, 2, and 3 encompass three AI-ready data center campuses in Northern Alberta designed to deliver an initial 220 MW of IT capacity. The multi-site development is currently in pre-construction."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>32. Nechalacho Basal Rare Earth Project</strong> ($2.84 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Nechalacho Basal Rare Earth Project in the Northwest Territories is an advanced-stage critical minerals development focusing on magnet-critical rare earth elements. The project is currently at the Preliminary Economic Assessment stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>31. Prairie Connector Project</strong> ($2.84 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Prairie Connector Project is a 380-kilometre pipeline expansion connecting Hardisty, Alberta, to the U.S. border. Commercial contracts were executed in early 2026, with a Final Investment Decision targeted for mid-2027."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>30. Strange Lake Rare Earth Project</strong> ($2.93 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Strange Lake Rare Earth Project is an integrated mine-to-separated-oxide critical minerals project spanning Québec and Labrador. A 2026 Feasibility Study is underway alongside targeted completion of its Environmental Impact Assessment."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>29. The Baptiste Nickel Project</strong> ($2.97 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Baptiste Nickel Project in British Columbia is a large-scale nickel development aimed at supplying high-grade nickel concentrate for battery materials, stainless steel, and specialty alloys. Following a 2023 pre-feasibility study, the project entered formal environmental assessment in early 2026."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>28. A. Murray MacKay Bridge</strong> ($3.00 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The A. Murray MacKay Bridge project targets the redevelopment or replacement of a critical Nova Scotia transit corridor handling over 20 million vehicle crossings annually. Feasibility studies and options analysis are ongoing through 2027/28 to select a preferred long-term solution."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>27. Clearwater Project</strong> ($3.33 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Clearwater Project is an Alberta lithium development using Direct Lithium Extraction technology to extract lithium from Leduc reservoir brines. It is currently undergoing demonstration testing, feasibility studies, and permitting."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>26. Kivalliq Hydro-Fibre Link (KHFL)</strong> ($3.40 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Kivalliq Hydro-Fibre Link is an Inuit-led nation-building project designed to connect Nunavut directly to North America’s electric grid and fibre-optic communications network. The initiative is currently in pre-construction."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>25. Casino Project</strong> ($3.90 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Casino Project in Yukon represents one of the world’s largest undeveloped copper-gold deposits and a major critical minerals resource. The development is currently undergoing environmental assessment through the YESAB Panel Review process."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>24. Prince Rupert Biofuels Project</strong> ($4.00 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Prince Rupert Biofuels Project in British Columbia is planned as Western North America’s first waterborne biofuels facility, targeting production of biomethanol, sustainable aviation fuel, green hydrogen, and biogenic CO2. It is currently in the pre-feasibility stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>23. Kami Iron Ore Mine Project</strong> ($4.01 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Kami Iron Ore Mine Project in Newfoundland and Labrador aims to produce 9 million wet metric tonnes per year of direct reduction grade pellet feed. Following a 2024 prefeasibility study, the project remains in the prefeasibility phase evaluating mine and processing infrastructure construction."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>22. Projet Mauricie</strong> ($4.05 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Projet Mauricie is a clean energy platform in Québec aimed at producing up to 70,000 tonnes of green hydrogen and 80,000 tonnes of renewable natural gas per year. The project is actively advancing through FEED and regulatory permitting."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>21. Berg Copper Project</strong> ($4.59 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Berg Copper Project is a proposed open-pit copper-molybdenum-silver mine located in central British Columbia. Having completed a Pre-Feasibility Study, the development is currently preparing its environmental assessment submissions."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>20. Spruce Lake Data Center Hub</strong> ($5.27 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spruce Lake Data Center Hub is a proposed 300-acre hyperscale and AI-focused campus in New Brunswick capable of delivering up to 365 MW of power. The greenfield project is currently in early development."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>19. Genesee Energy Campus</strong> ($5.40 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Genesee Energy Campus project involves expanding an existing energy site near Edmonton into a major power generation and energy storage hub. The project is currently in the pre-construction phase."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>18. Nova Scotia Renewable Energy Park</strong> ($6.08 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Nova Scotia Renewable Energy Park is an integrated sustainable aviation fuel (SAF) and renewable methanol facility engineered to output 165,000 tonnes of SAF per year. The project has successfully completed its pre-FEED phase."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>17. PowerCo Canada</strong> ($6.75 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "PowerCo Canada is constructing a world-scale battery cell manufacturing facility in St. Thomas, Ontario, serving as a critical node for the North American battery supply chain. First-phase construction is underway as site development and major contract awards advance."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>16. The North Atlantic Green Energy Hub</strong> ($6.89 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The North Atlantic Green Energy Hub is a wind-powered green hydrogen initiative in Newfoundland and Labrador designed to produce 90,000 tonnes of RFNBO-compliant hydrogen annually for international export. It is in advanced development and transitioning into the FEED stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>15. Telesat Lightspeed LEO Constellation</strong> ($7.02 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Telesat Lightspeed project is a satellite network consisting of 225 low Earth orbit satellites designed to deliver global broadband coverage starting in Q1 2028. The initiative is in advanced development and early manufacturing."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>14. Mactaquac Life Achievement Project</strong> ($7.9 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Mactaquac Life Achievement Project is a major brownfield hydroelectric infrastructure endeavor aimed at extending the operating lifespan of New Brunswick’s 668 MW Mactaquac Generating Station. The project is actively in the engineering and design phase."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>13. Commercial Low-Carbon-Intensity Synthetic Gasoline</strong> ($8.78 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This project entails constructing two commercial-scale Blue Gasoline facilities in Alberta that convert natural gas into low-carbon synthetic gasoline using carbon capture technology. Currently in advanced development and FEED-ready, preferred sites have been identified as commercial and engineering activities progress toward a Final Investment Decision."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>12. AI Hub of Innovation</strong> ($9 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The AI Hub of Innovation is a central Alberta project integrating an AI data center with behind-the-meter power generation, structured to scale from 300 MW to 1 GW. Construction is underway, with initial power delivery targeted for late 2026."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>11. Prairie Link High Speed Rail</strong> ($10.9 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prairie Link High Speed Rail is a proposed passenger rail line between Calgary and Edmonton capable of speeds exceeding 300 km/h and transit times of around 90 minutes. It is currently in Stage 3 of its Project Development Agreement, with a Final Investment Decision targeted for December 2027."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>10. Woodfibre LNG Expansion</strong> ($13.37 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Woodfibre project is a concept phase debottlenecking project that would increase the facility’s production capacity while leveraging existing infrastructure and utilities."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>9. The EVREC Green Energy Hub</strong> ($14.31 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The EVREC Green Energy Hub is an integrated green hydrogen and renewable ammonia facility in Newfoundland and Labrador that combines wind, solar, battery storage, electrolysis, and export infrastructure. It is currently in the FEED stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>8. Redcliff AI Data Center Campus</strong> ($14.50 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Redcliff AI Data Center Campus is a proposed 480-acre greenfield AI compute hub near Medicine Hat, Alberta, featuring 884 MW of behind-the-meter power generation. The project is currently undergoing front-end engineering design (FEED) and environmental permitting."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>7. TransPod Alberta Corridor</strong> ($22.00 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The TransPod Alberta Corridor is an ultra-high-speed transit project designed to connect Calgary and Edmonton with zero direct emissions, moving passengers and cargo at speeds over 1,000 km/h. It is currently under a Request for Expression of Interest review with the Alberta government."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>6. Kino Aski LNG</strong> ($31.05 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kino Aski LNG is a proposed cross-Canada LNG export project that seeks to connect Western Canadian natural gas supplies to an export facility at the Port of Baie-Comeau in Québec. The project is currently in its Pre-Application stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>5. West Coast Oil Pipeline</strong> ($35.00 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The West Coast Oil Pipeline is a proposed 1,250-kilometre pipeline and marine terminal aimed at connecting Alberta crude production directly to a deepwater export facility in southern British Columbia. The project is in early development, with a Final Investment Decision targeted between 2027 and 2029."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>4. Ksi Lisims LNG Terminal</strong> ($38.48 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Co-developed by the Nisga’a Nation, Rockies LNG, and Western LNG, Ksi Lisims LNG is a planned 12 MTPA floating LNG export facility in northern British Columbia. The project has secured key environmental clearances and major offtake agreements as it moves through advanced permitting toward being shovel-ready."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>3. Novatron Energy</strong> ($48.60 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Novatron Energy is a clean energy platform that combines approximately 10 GW of onshore and offshore wind generation with two HVDC transmission corridors connecting Atlantic Canada to Quebec. The project is currently in its primary development stage."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>2. Wind West</strong> ($59.40 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wind West is a proposed offshore wind and transmission development in Nova Scotia designed to deliver up to 5 GW of offshore wind generation and roughly 24 TWh of clean electricity annually. The initiative is currently progressing through early feasibility and pre-development activities."}, {"k": "h3", "t": "<strong>1. Port of Churchill Plus</strong> ($76.95 Billion)"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Port of Churchill Plus is a major infrastructure initiative designed to transform Manitoba’s Port of Churchill into a four-season trade gateway through an LNG export corridor, rail upgrades, port modernization, and new all-weather road access. The project is advancing through ongoing expansion, engineering, and feasibility activities."}]}, {"id": "nexgen", "tag": "Projects", "topic": "Projects", "title": "NexGen breaks ground on $2.2B uranium mine in Saskatchewan", "dek": "Construction begins on NexGen’s Rook I project, adding a major underground uranium development to Saskatchewan’s nuclear-fuel industry.", "homeDek": "Construction begins on NexGen’s Rook I project, adding a major underground uranium development to Saskatchewan’s nuclear-fuel industry.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Aug 14, 2026", "img": "nexgen", "alt": "NexGen breaks ground on $2.2B uranium mine in Saskatchewan", "credit": "Unsplash", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/nexgen-breaks-ground-on-2-2b-uranium-mine-in-saskatchewan/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["Crews have broken ground on NexGen Energy’s $2.2 billion Rook 1 project in Saskatchewan, which is expected to take four years to build and could produce roughly 20% of the world’s uranium supply.", "The mine uses traditional long-hole stoping in solid basement rock and features an underground tailings facility where process waste is returned underground as cemented paste rather than kept in surface ponds.", "Over an estimated 11 year mine life, the project is projected to create hundreds of direct jobs, generate significant government revenues, and strengthen Canada’s position as a major supplier of nuclear fuel."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crews in Northern Saskatchewan have officially broken ground on what could be the world’s biggest uranium mine ever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "NexGen Energy’s $2.2B Rook 1 underground mine is being constructed in the southwestern portion of the Athabasca Basin along Patterson Lake, about 640 kilometres northwest of Saskatoon, near the Alberta border. Work on Rook I is expected to take four years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once complete, experts say it could represent roughly 20% of the world’s uranium supply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The project includes facilities to support the extraction and processing of uranium ore from the region’s Arrow Deposit. NexGen has conducted exploration, geotechnical, and environmental and community studies since 2013 to define the resource and plan operations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sinking two main vertical shafts into the earth establishes access to the ore body, with a primary shaft for hoisting material and intake air alongside an exhaust shaft for ventilation and utility routing. Unlike historical Athabasca Basin mines that traverse water-saturated sandstone and demand permanent artificial ground freezing, the Arrow Deposit rests entirely within solid crystalline basement rock starting roughly 100 meters below the surface. This competent rock structure allows crew members to deploy traditional long-hole stoping methods without the continuous power footprint or geotechnical complexity of freeze plants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once extracted, the ore transfers to a streamlined surface processing mill designed for a throughput capacity of roughly 1,400 tonnes of ore per day. The monometallic nature of the Arrow ore means the chemical extraction circuit requires far fewer complex separation steps than typical polymetallic deposits, simplifying the overall footprint of the mill building. Water handling infrastructure and multi-stage effluent treatment facilities surround the plant to manage process fluids before discharge back into the environment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perhaps the most defining structural engineering feature of the Rook I build is its Underground Tailings Management Facility (UGTMF). Rather than creating a conventional surface tailings pond or dam, construction plans dictate that 100% of processed tailings are returned underground as a cemented paste. This material is pumped into mined-out stopes and dedicated underground storage chambers, backfilling the voids to ensure long-term ground stability while completely eliminating the risk of surface dam failures or long-term surface tailings maintenance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Supporting the primary mine and mill site is a comprehensive surface infrastructure layout built to handle remote northern Saskatchewan logistics. The four-year construction effort involves assembling an on-site permanent accommodations camp, dedicated power generation facilities, fuel storage tanks, waste rock storage pads, access roads, and an airstrip to ferry crew and materials roughly 155 km north of La Loche."}, {"k": "p", "t": "NexGen’s feasibility study predicts an 11 year mine life and production of 233.6 million pounds of recovered yellowcake from Probable Mineral Reserves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Officials say mine provides a major financial engine for both Saskatchewan and Canada, generating hundreds of millions of dollars annually in tax revenues and royalties while creating roughly 350 direct jobs during construction and nearly 500 long-term operational positions, with hiring prioritized for local and Indigenous communities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "estimated 20% of global uranium demand strengthens Canada’s geopolitical stature as a premier, secure supplier of nuclear fuel in an era of heightened energy security concerns. It also directly supports domestic and international net-zero climate goals by providing the essential clean-energy feedstock required to power zero-emission nuclear energy generation worldwide."}]}, {"id": "edacq", "tag": "People", "topic": "People", "title": "EllisDon acquires two power infrastructure firms", "dek": "Hardline Engineering and Firefly Power & Energy join EllisDon, strengthening its ability to design, construct and maintain electrical infrastructure.", "homeDek": "Hardline Engineering and Firefly Power & Energy join EllisDon, strengthening its ability to design, construct and maintain electrical infrastructure.", "author": "Brett Rutledge", "av": 0, "date": "Sep 1, 2026", "img": "edacq", "alt": "EllisDon acquires two power infrastructure firms", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/ellisdon-acquires-two-power-infrastructure-firms/", "b": [{"k": "p", "t": "EllisDon has acquired Hardline Engineering and Firefly Power & Energy, expanding its power infrastructure capabilities across engineering, construction, and utility operations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The acquisitions strengthen EllisDon’s expertise in high- and medium-voltage power engineering, transmission and distribution systems, substation infrastructure, and utility construction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hardline Engineering provides engineering and project delivery services across interconnections, transmission, distribution, substations, and streetlighting. Firefly Power & Energy specializes in high-voltage construction, operations, and maintenance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“We’re excited to welcome the Hardline and Firefly teams to EllisDon,” said Ian Kerr, Senior Vice President and Area Manager, EllisDon Power. “They have built exceptional businesses and earned the trust of clients across the industry. Together, we’re bringing integrated solutions to the power sector, strengthening our ability to deliver power projects that support growing communities and industries across Canada.”"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both companies will continue operating under their established brands while benefiting from EllisDon’s resources and scale."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The move positions EllisDon to support electric utility, renewable energy, industrial, commercial, and public-sector clients across the full project lifecycle — from engineering and design through construction, delivery, operations, and maintenance."}]}, {"id": "pearson", "tag": "Projects", "topic": "Projects", "title": "PCL, NORR-DIALOG win $1.5-billion Toronto airport overhaul", "dek": "Toronto Pearson selects PCL and NORR-DIALOG to modernize two terminals while maintaining airport operations through staged construction.", "homeDek": "Toronto Pearson selects PCL and NORR-DIALOG to modernize two terminals while maintaining airport operations through staged construction.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Jul 22, 2026", "img": "pearson", "alt": "PCL, NORR-DIALOG win $1.5-billion Toronto airport overhaul", "credit": "Unsplash", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/pcl-norr-dialog-win-1-5-billion-toronto-airport-overhaul/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["Toronto Pearson has awarded a $1.5-billion contract to NORR-DIALOG (Design) and PCL Construction (Construction Manager at Risk) to modernize Terminals 1 and 3.", "Upgrades will target passenger flow, including modernized check-in areas, high-throughput security screening, customs facilities, and refreshed terminal lounges.", "Construction will be executed in staged phases to keep Canada’s busiest airport operating 24/7 without flight or passenger disruptions.", "The revitalization is designed to support 7 million additional annual passengers by 2031, preparing the airport for a long-term goal of 65 million annual travelers."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greater Toronto Airports Authority (GTAA) has officially awarded a $1.5-billion contract to launch the Terminal 1 and Terminal 3 Revitalization Program at Toronto Pearson International Airport. Design and architecture will be led by NORR-DIALOG—a joint venture between Ontario-based design firms NORR and DIALOG—while PCL Construction will serve as the Construction Manager at Risk to manage complex on-site execution."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The major modernization drive marks the second phase of “Pearson LIFT” (Long-term Investment in Facilities and Terminals), a multi-billion-dollar infrastructure initiative.<sup></sup> The project follows a $3-billion investment in airside and baggage logistics launched earlier this year.<sup></sup> Engineered to expand overall facility capacity and streamline passenger processing, the program includes expanded terminal footprints, modernized check-in zones, advanced security screening, updated customs facilities, and refreshed airline lounges.<sup></sup>"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To prevent travel disruptions at Canada’s busiest airport, construction will be phased carefully in a live 24/7 operating environment.<sup></sup> The upgrades will support up to seven million additional annual travelers by 2031, positioning Pearson to accommodate long-term demand of up to 65 million passengers.<sup></sup>"}, {"k": "p", "t": "“Toronto Pearson, one of Canada’s most important national infrastructure assets, serves as a vital gateway connecting people, products, and businesses to the world,” said Deborah Flint, President and CEO of Toronto Pearson. “This investment marks another significant milestone in Pearson LIFT and demonstrates our momentum in modernizing Pearson that will meet customer expectations. We’re elevating the travel experience so Toront<sup></sup>o Pearson can continue its part in fueling Canada’s economy by welcoming more passenger growth and o<sup></sup>ffering better customer experience.”<sup></sup>"}, {"k": "p", "t": "PCL Constructors Canada Vice President Monique Buckberger emphasized the operational focus required for the build, noting that delivering the project in a live, 24/7 operating airport environment makes maintaining safe and uninterrupted operations for passengers, airlines, and airport partners critical. She added that PCL’s primary focus will be executing carefully phased construction that keeps the airport operating efficiently while minimizing impacts for passengers and the broader airport community."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Terminal 1 and Terminal 3 revitalization project serves as a cornerstone within the broader, multi-billion-dollar Pearson LIFT strategy, an ambitious decade-long initiative designed to modernize Canada’s largest transportation hub.<sup></sup> The overall transformation is divided into distinct execution phases aimed at scaling the airport’s infrastructure from its current capacity up to 65 million annual travelers by the early 2030s.<sup></sup>"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first phase, which broke ground earlier in 2026 under a $3-billion capital commitment, focuses on essential airside upgrades, high-speed airfield taxiways, and automated subsurface baggage network technologies.<sup></sup>"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This newly awarded $1.5-billion second phase shifts the focus directly to the passenger experience inside the terminal buildings.<sup></sup> Led by PCL Construction and the NORR-DIALOG design team, the program will modernize aging infrastructure across both Terminal 1 and Terminal 3. Key improvements include expanding physical terminal spaces, upgrading customs and border processing zones, installing high-throughput security screening lanes, adding in-terminal device charging stations, and renovating airline lounges to ensure a seamless flow from the curb to the gate.<sup></sup>"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Looking ahead, future phases of the LIFT framework—such as the planned “Gateway” expansion—will add brand-new terminal floor space to accommodate an additional 15 million passengers per year, completing the airport’s long-term master plan.<sup></sup>"}]}, {"id": "atco", "tag": "Projects", "topic": "Projects", "title": "ATCO breaks ground in Alberta on its largest project ever", "dek": "Work starts on the Yellowhead Pipeline, a major investment intended to increase natural gas delivery into Alberta’s Industrial Heartland.", "homeDek": "Work starts on the Yellowhead Pipeline, a major investment intended to increase natural gas delivery into Alberta’s Industrial Heartland.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Oct 14, 2026", "img": "atco", "alt": "ATCO breaks ground in Alberta on its largest project ever", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/atco-breaks-ground-on-its-largest-project-ever/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["Canadian Utilities has commenced construction on the $2.9-billion, 235-kilometre Yellowhead natural gas pipeline between Peers and Fort Saskatchewan.", "The project will deliver 1.1 billion cubic feet of natural gas per day, with 100 percent of its transport capacity fully subscribed prior to regulatory approval.", "Construction is expected to create 2,000 direct jobs, targeting an in-service operational date by late 2027 to unlock $3.9 billion in annual downstream GDP growth."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "ATCO has officially broken ground on the $2.9-billion Yellowhead Pipeline, marking the start of construction on the largest project in the company’s history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The 235-kilometre natural gas pipeline will run from Peers, Alberta, to Fort Saskatchewan, delivering 1.1 billion cubic feet of natural gas per day into Alberta’s Industrial Heartland. The additional transportation capacity is designed to resolve regional system constraints while supporting population growth, industrial development, and expanded gas-fired power generation. Commercial demand for the pipeline’s full capacity was secured prior to completing the regulatory review process."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Construction of the pipeline is expected to generate approximately 2,000 direct jobs. Once operational, the energy infrastructure is projected to enable downstream industrial investments that could support an estimated 12,000 construction roles, 20,000 permanent operational jobs, and add $3.9 billion annually to Alberta’s GDP."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“Yellowhead represents a major investment in Alberta’s future and a demonstration of Canadian Utilities’ ability to plan, finance, permit, construct and operate large-scale infrastructure that our customers and economy depend upon,” said Bob Myles, Chief Executive Officer of Canadian Utilities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jason Sharpe, Chief Operating Officer of ATCO Energy Systems, emphasized the collaborative development of the project: “As the largest project in our company’s history, Yellowhead represents more than a pipeline… We’re energizing Alberta’s next chapter thanks to the support of our industry partners, suppliers, contractors, Indigenous partners and community leaders.”"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The groundbreaking follows formal project approval by the Alberta Utilities Commission (AUC) in July 2026. Pre-construction activities are currently underway, with the pipeline targeted to enter commercial service by late 2027."}]}, {"id": "gordie", "tag": "Projects", "topic": "Projects", "title": "Gordie Howe Bridge opens with Canadian-only ceremony", "dek": "Canadian officials mark completion of the Windsor–Detroit crossing without U.S. counterparts, following a dispute over the project’s financial arrangements.", "homeDek": "Canadian officials mark completion of the Windsor–Detroit crossing without U.S. counterparts, following a dispute over the project’s financial arrangements.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Jul 24, 2026", "img": "gordie", "alt": "Gordie Howe Bridge opens with Canadian-only ceremony", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/gordie-howe-bridge-celebrates-opening-with-canadian-only-ceremony/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["The bridge was officially inaugurated in a Canadian-only ceremony following tension with the U.S. over a renegotiated financial agreement that forces Canada to split net revenues evenly for 15 years.", "The $6.4-billion public-private partnership project was built by the Bridging North America consortium, requiring 20.1 million trade hours from 15,800 workers since breaking ground in 2018.", "Featuring a continental-record 853-metre main cable-stayed span and 220-metre towers, the six-lane crossing will open to vehicular traffic on July 27 to handle over $274 million in daily cross-border trade."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Canadian officials gathered near the banks of the Detroit River on Friday to officially inaugurate the long-awaited Gordie Howe International Bridge, holding a ribbon-cutting ceremony and exhibition hockey game without their American counterparts present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The celebration marked the formal end of construction on the massive infrastructure project connecting Windsor, Ont., to Detroit, Mich., ahead of its scheduled opening to vehicular traffic on Monday. However, the absence of U.S. officials cast a shadow over what Ottawa had long framed as a landmark triumph of binational collaboration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Canadian-only inauguration follows weeks of tense geopolitical friction stirred by U.S. President Donald Trump, who threatened to block border operations unless the financial terms governing the bridge were drastically renegotiated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under a newly released “Proposed Agreement in Principle” published by Transport Canada late this week, Ottawa shifted how the multi-billion-dollar project will pay for itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "While Canada entirely financed the $6.4-billion design and construction, the new terms dictate that Canada must share 50% of all net bridge revenues directly with the United States for the first 15 years of operation. The agreement eliminates Canada’s ability to collect 100% of toll revenues until its construction debt is paid off. Furthermore, the U.S. government now holds veto power over future toll rates, and its half of the net revenue will be directed into an economic development fund solely controlled by Washington."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Despite the drama, the project stands as a triumph of heavy civil engineering. Built by public-private partner Bridging North America — a consortium comprising ACS Infrastructure Canada, Fluor, and Aecon, alongside lead designers Dragados, Fluor, and AECOM — it required over 20.1 million trade hours  across nearly 8 years. Since breaking ground in October 2018, more than 15,800 workers participated in driving thousands of deep foundation piles, erecting massive concrete towers, and tensioning 216 stay cables to link the two nations."}, {"k": "fig", "src": "f_gordie-1", "cap": "", "alt": ""}, {"k": "p", "t": "From a structural standpoint, the bridge is impressive. Resting on 220-metre-tall towers, it boasts the longest cable-stayed main span on the continent at 853 metres, providing a clear 42-metre navigation clearance over the Detroit River. Built to a 125-year design life, the project utilized tens of thousands of tonnes of structural steel and high-strength concrete across its six lanes, multi-use path, and the two largest land border inspection ports along the entire Canada-U.S. border."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Conceived more than two decades ago to relieve chronic gridlock at the privately owned Ambassador Bridge and the Windsor-Detroit Tunnel, the Gordie Howe project represents the first new crossing between Ontario and Michigan in over 60 years. The Windsor-Detroit corridor remains Canada’s busiest commercial land border point, carrying roughly 30% of all Canada-U.S. truck trade valued at more than $274 million daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“After years of planning, partnership, and construction, the Gordie Howe International Bridge will soon be opening — providing a new connection for the region, while strengthening one of the world’s most important trade corridors,” Infrastructure Minister Gregor Robertson said in a statement. “This nation-building project is a testament to what Canada can accomplish when we come together with a shared vision.”"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ontario Premier Doug Ford noted that the bridge will serve as a vital lifeline for manufacturing and supply chains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“This bridge is a testament to the incredible legacy of Gordie Howe, and to the millions of Americans and Canadians who work together every day to make both of our countries more secure, safe and prosperous,” Ford said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Canada Border Services Agency (CBSA) confirmed that its new 24-lane facility, equipped with 12 commercial examination docks and advanced imaging technology, is fully staffed and operational."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“With state-of-the-art infrastructure, advanced technology and a highly trained workforce supporting operations at the site, the Agency is ready to deliver safe, efficient and reliable border services now and into the future,” said CBSA president Erin O’Gorman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Commercial and passenger vehicles will be permitted to cross the bridge starting at 12:00 p.m. on Monday, July 27. A toll-free multi-use path for pedestrians and cyclists, integrated into the Trans Canada Trail network, is scheduled to open on Aug. 5."}]}, {"id": "prgt", "tag": "Projects", "topic": "Projects", "title": "$10B B.C. pipeline project awards land works to Canadian team", "dek": "Surerus Murphy secures the first terrestrial construction agreement for Prince Rupert Gas Transmission, advancing the pipeline’s land-based delivery program.", "homeDek": "Surerus Murphy secures the first terrestrial construction agreement for Prince Rupert Gas Transmission, advancing the pipeline’s land-based delivery program.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Aug 12, 2026", "img": "prgt", "alt": "$10B B.C. pipeline project awards land works to Canadian team", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/10b-b-c-pipeline-project-awards-land-works-to-canadian-team/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["The pipeline project has an estimated construction cost of $10 billion.", "The original route was shortened from 900 kilometers to 750 kilometers and redirected away from Prince Rupert to feed the proposed Ksi Lisims LNG export facility.", "Ownership was acquired in 2024 by a joint venture between Western LNG and the Nisga’a Nation, with 15 out of 20 First Nations along the corridor signing equity agreements.", "The project’s marine pipeline work was recently awarded to Swiss subsea specialist Allseas. International engineering firm Bechtel is leading overall execution planning and contractor selection."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Prince Rupert Gas Transmission Project (PRGT) has awarded its first terrestrial pipeline construction agreement to Surerus Murphy Joint Venture (SMJV), establishing an all-Canadian contractor lineup for the land-based segments of the natural gas pipeline route."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Formed in 2015, Surerus Murphy is a joint venture between Surerus Pipeline Inc., a family-owned company based in Fort St. John, B.C., and J. Murphy & Sons, which is headquartered in the UK with Canadian offices in Calgary and Edmonton. Formed in 2015, Surerus Murphy combines more than 100 years of pipeline construction experience and has built major energy infrastructure projects across western Canada."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acquired from TC Energy in 2024 by the Nisga’a Nation and Western LNG, the PRGT pipeline will transport natural gas from northeastern B.C. to the proposed Ksi Lisims LNG export facility on B.C.’s northwest coast. Initial construction began on Nisga’a Lands following the acquisition, establishing a co-owned model for Indigenous-led energy infrastructure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“Focusing on Canadian construction companies like SMJV for the land sections of PRGT is a deliberate decision,” said Eva Clayton, President of Nisga’a Lisims Government. “When we build with Canadian companies, and those companies subcontract to local and Indigenous businesses, that means huge benefits stay right here in British Columbia.”"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mick Fitzpatrick, President of SMJV, emphasized the team’s regional experience: “As an energy infrastructure construction company rooted in Western Canada, we understand the uniqueness of this project scope intimately, and we know we can use our experience to complete this work safely and responsibly.” Western LNG CEO Davis Thames added that SMJV’s track record in northern B.C. ensures “fewer surprises and faster problem-solving.”"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The terrestrial contract with SMJV follows the project’s marine pipeline award to Swiss subsea specialist Allseas. International engineering firm Bechtel is leading overall execution planning and contractor selection for the project."}]}, {"id": "rbc", "tag": "Economy", "topic": "Economy", "title": "RBC predicts $200B project boom by 2040", "dek": "An RBC–McKinsey report outlines substantial energy investment potential, alongside labour and delivery risks that could constrain the construction pipeline.", "homeDek": "An RBC–McKinsey report outlines substantial energy investment potential, alongside labour and delivery risks that could constrain the construction pipeline.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Oct 7, 2026", "img": "rbc", "alt": "RBC predicts $200B project boom by 2040", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/rbc-predicts-200b-project-boom-by-2040/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["A new RBC-McKinsey study projects $200 billion in energy capital expenditure through 2040, driven by LNG expansions, new crude pipelines, and carbon-capture infrastructure.", "High-growth projections forecast a two-million-barrel-per-day increase in Canadian oil output and a near-doubling of natural gas production, adding $44 billion annually to national GDP.", "B.C. and Alberta construction spending is expected to peak at up to $31 billion annually around 2030, requiring up to 132,000 workers amid tightening skilled trades shortages."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A new joint report by RBC Thought Leadership and McKinsey & Company indicates Canada is on the verge of a major oil and gas expansion that could drive more than $200 billion in capital expenditure through 2040."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under a high-growth “Step Change” scenario outlined in the study, Canadian oil production could increase by two million barrels per day by 2040, while natural gas output could nearly double. The expansion is projected to boost Canada’s oil and gas GDP by nearly 50%, adding $44 billion annually to real GDP and generating 52,100 permanent production jobs in Alberta alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The report highlights an upcoming wave of major project decisions expected between late 2026 and mid-2027. Key developments include final investment decisions for LNG Canada Phase 2 and Ksi Lisims LNG, South Bow’s Prairie Connector pipeline, the Pacific Link crude oil pipeline, and binding agreements for the Pathways Alliance carbon-capture network. Construction activity across Alberta and British Columbia is projected to peak between 2029 and 2030 at $25 billion to $31 billion annually, requiring an estimated peak workforce of 109,000 to 132,000 construction and supply chain workers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Export diversification to Asian markets represents a major economic driver of the expansion. With the completion of new West Coast LNG terminals and the Pacific Link pipeline, non-U.S. oil and gas exports could rise from $10 billion in 2024 to $100 billion annually by 2040. The report notes that establishing a second major crude export corridor to Asia could narrow the Western Canadian Select (WCS) heavy oil discount against U.S. benchmarks by up to US$3 per barrel, generating up to $7 billion in annual national economic value."}, {"k": "p", "t": "However, the analysis warns of significant execution challenges, noting that all eight Western Canadian energy megaprojects over the past two build cycles exceeded their original budgets and schedules. The authors identify acute risks surrounding trade certification shortages—particularly among pipefitters, welders, millwrights, and boilermakers—alongside domestic supply chain limitations, given that Canada currently maintains only one large-diameter line-pipe mill in Regina and a single steel plate producer in Ontario."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To prevent cost overruns and capacity bottlenecks, the report recommends establishing an intergovernmental “clearing house” table to sequence major projects, alongside policy measures to encourage domestic steel fabrication and support climate-tech commercialization around carbon capture and methane reduction."}]}, {"id": "pclott", "tag": "Projects", "topic": "Projects", "title": "PCL wins $768M Ottawa science facility contract", "dek": "PCL secures construction work on Ottawa’s RSS Main campus, a major component of the federal program to renew scientific facilities.", "homeDek": "PCL secures construction work on Ottawa’s RSS Main campus, a major component of the federal program to renew scientific facilities.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Aug 10, 2026", "img": "pclott", "alt": "PCL wins $768M Ottawa science facility contract", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/pcl-wins-768m-ottawa-science-facility-contract/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["Ottawa has awarded a $768.6-million construction contract to PCL Construction Canada Inc. for the $1-billion Phase 1 build of the RSS Main science hub.", "Part of the $3.8-billion Laboratories Canada initiative, the campus will feature six green-certified facilities housing 275 scientists across CFIA, CBSA, Health Canada, and PHAC.", "The project bolsters border protection, food safety, and public health while generating $900 million toward Canada’s NATO defense resilience targets."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Public Services and Procurement Canada has awarded a $768.6-million construction services contract to PCL Construction Canada Inc. to build the Regulatory and Security Science Main (RSS Main) facility in Ottawa. The award marks a major transition into full structural construction on the Canadian Food Inspection Agency’s existing Ottawa Laboratory site, following preliminary site preparation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The project represents the anchor phase of the federal government’s broader $3.8-billion Laboratories Canada strategy to overhaul aging federal research infrastructure. Valued at approximately $1 billion, this initial build accounts for roughly half of the total planned footprint for the RSS Main campus. Designed using standardized, repeatable laboratory architecture, the new complex is targeting LEED and Fitwel certifications for sustainability and workplace wellness, with full construction expected to wrap up in 2031."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once complete, the campus will feature six modern science facilities housing approximately 275 federal scientists and researchers supporting 25 inter-agency programs. The hub is built to break down departmental silos, uniting researchers from the Canadian Food Inspection Agency, Canada Border Services Agency, Health Canada, and Public Health Agency of Canada under one roof. Beyond public health and food safety, the facility plays a critical strategic role: through combined construction, operations, and personnel costs over the next decade, the project will contribute roughly $900 million toward Canada’s defense and resilience spending under NATO’s investment framework."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“Canadians rely on science every day, whether it’s protecting public health, strengthening our borders, or helping Canada respond to emerging risks and challenges,” said Joël Lightbound, Minister of Government Transformation, Public Works and Procurement. “Through Laboratories Canada, we are making one of the largest investments in federal science infrastructure in a generation. RSS Main will equip Canada’s scientists and researchers with the modern tools they need to tackle emerging challenges, strengthen Canada’s resilience, support economic growth, and help keep Canadians safe for years to come.”"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Defense Minister David J. McGuinty highlighted the local and national impact, noting that the city’s role as an innovation hub is directly reinforced by the investment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "“Ottawa is home to some of Canada’s most important scientific and research institutions, and this investment reinforces our city’s role as a leader in innovation, public service, and national resilience,” McGuinty said. “The new RSS Main campus will bring together world-class scientists and researchers in modern facilities, creating good jobs, advancing Canadian expertise, and strengthening the capabilities that help keep Canadians safe and our economy strong.”"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To maximize domestic economic impact, procurement follows “Buy Canadian” directives that prioritize Canadian-sourced steel, wood, and aluminum. The federal government is also partnering with the Anishinàbe Algonquin Nation and other Indigenous partners to incorporate Indigenous design perspectives and establish Indigenous Participation Plans that create direct commercial opportunities for local businesses."}]}, {"id": "gip", "tag": "Economy", "topic": "Economy", "title": "GIP expands Prairie footprint with acquisitions", "dek": "GIP adds Saskatchewan paving and fleet-service businesses, combining regional civil construction experience with equipment maintenance capabilities.", "homeDek": "GIP adds Saskatchewan paving and fleet-service businesses, combining regional civil construction experience with equipment maintenance capabilities.", "author": "Russell Hixson", "av": 1, "date": "Jul 24, 2026", "img": "gip", "alt": "GIP expands Prairie footprint with acquisitions", "credit": "Supplied", "src": "https://readsitenews.com/gip-expands-prairie-footprint-with-acquisitions/", "b": [{"k": "kt", "items": ["Green Infrastructure Partners (GIP) has acquired two separate companies—Potzus Paving & Road Maintenance Ltd. and Truck Saver Service (Yorkton) Ltd.—which will operate under the unified banner of GIP Potzus Paving Ltd.", "Potzus Paving brings over six decades of heavy civil engineering, aggregate crushing, earthmoving, and asphalt paving experience in Saskatchewan.", "Truck Saver Service adds specialized fleet maintenance and mechanical service capabilities to ensure equipment uptime across regional project sites."]}, {"k": "ws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Green Infrastructure Partners Inc. (GIP) has formally expanded its civil construction and service footprint across Western Canada through the dual acquisition of two Yorkton-based businesses: civil contractor Potzus Paving & Road Maintenance Ltd. and heavy equipment maintenance provider Truck Saver Service (Yorkton) Ltd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Officially joining GIP’s operational network in July 2026 as GIP Potzus Paving Ltd., the integration brings together Potzus’s 60-year history in heavy civil infrastructure with Truck Saver Service’s dedicated mechanical and fleet maintenance expertise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In an official statement welcoming both teams, GIP emphasized that the acquisitions reflect a shared commitment to craftsmanship, safety, and regional collaboration. The company noted that adding these established Saskatchewan businesses strengthens its ability to build connected communities and deliver vertically integrated infrastructure solutions across Western Canada."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The integration of Potzus Paving & Road Maintenance Ltd. and Truck Saver Service (Yorkton) Ltd. marks a strategic step in scaling GIP’s Prairie operations:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["<strong>Potzus Paving & Road Maintenance Ltd.:</strong> A second-generation, family-owned civil construction leader established in 1966. The company brings a specialized workforce of over 150 professionals, three aggregate crushing plants, and three asphalt plants to support major highway, earthmoving, and paving contracts across Saskatchewan and Manitoba.", "<strong>Truck Saver Service (Yorkton) Ltd.:</strong> An independent commercial vehicle and heavy equipment service specialist based in Yorkton. Its mechanical maintenance and repair capabilities bolster fleet reliability and reduce operational downtime for heavy civil projects."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Headquartered in Yorkton, Saskatchewan, Potzus Paving has built a 60-year legacy delivering heavy civil infrastructure, highway maintenance, earthmoving, and paving services for municipal, provincial, and commercial clients throughout Saskatchewan and Manitoba. By integrating Potzus alongside its heavy maintenance division, Truck Saver Service, GIP strengthens its vertically integrated supply chain, self-perform capacity, and regional equipment fleet across the Prairie provinces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a statement announcing the merger, GIP highlighted that the transaction underscores a shared commitment to quality craftsmanship, safety, and regional collaboration. The company emphasized that bringing the Yorkton team into the GIP family enhances its overall capacity to build connected communities, support long-term economic development, and deliver comprehensive infrastructure solutions across Western Canada."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The establishment of GIP Potzus Paving Ltd. marks another strategic step in expanding GIP’s civil infrastructure footprint throughout Western Canada. Headquartered in Yorkton with secondary operations in Saskatoon, the Potzus team brings a specialized workforce of over 150 skilled professionals, three aggregate crushing plants, and three asphalt plants into GIP’s network."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Combining Potzus’s local asphalt production, paving, and earthworks expertise with Truck Saver Service’s dedicated mechanical fleet maintenance capabilities allows GIP to streamline project delivery across Saskatchewan. The integrated platform will support major highway upgrades, municipal paving contracts, site development, and heavy industrial civil projects across the region while leveraging GIP’s broader national resources."}]}];
